## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of Linux networking and production stability for data-heavy services, combining NIC topology, traffic isolation, and programmable packet processing.

## Key Concepts
- NIC multi-queue configuration and mqprio
- tc clsact and eBPF-based QoS/policing
- XDP fast-path for critical traffic
- net_cls tagging and CPU isolation
- End-to-end validation with iperf/netperf and DB latency probes

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal eBPF skeleton to tag and count replication traffic
#include <linux/bpf.h>
#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>

SEC("xdp")
int xdp_prog(struct xdp_md *ctx) {
  // tag and potentially redirect/modify critical traffic
  return XDP_PASS;
}

char _license[] SEC("license") = "GPL";
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor and adapt QoS rules in response to changing workload patterns?
- What failure modes would force a rollback, and what automation would you put in place?

## Why This Is Asked
